1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,050
Part 3

2
00:00:01,050 --> 00:00:01,933
Annie:

3
00:00:01,933 --> 00:00:02,335
OK, Jack.

4
00:00:02,335 --> 00:00:08,840
Before we plan our presentation about refrigeration, let's discuss what we've discovered so far.

5
00:00:08,840 --> 00:00:08,890
Jack:

6
00:00:08,890 --> 00:00:09,624
Fine, Annie.

7
00:00:09,624 --> 00:00:12,500
Though I have to admit I haven't done much research yet.

8
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:12,927
Annie:

9
00:00:12,927 --> 00:00:13,782
Nor me.

10
00:00:13,782 --> 00:00:17,200
But I found an interesting article about icehouses.

11
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:21,267
I'd seen some 18th- and 19th-century ones here in the UK,

12
00:00:21,267 --> 00:00:25,333
so I knew they were often built in a shady area

13
00:00:25,333 --> 00:00:29,400
or underground, close to lakes that might freeze in the winter.

14
00:00:29,400 --> 00:00:34,064
Then blocks of ice could be cut and stored in the icehouse.

15
00:00:34,064 --> 00:00:37,951
But I didn't realise that insulating the blocks with straw

16
00:00:37,951 --> 00:00:41,060
or sawdust meant they didn't melt for months.

17
00:00:41,060 --> 00:00:43,890
The ancient Romans had refrigeration, too.

18
00:00:43,890 --> 00:00:44,090
Jack:

19
00:00:44,090 --> 00:00:44,890
I didn't know that.

20
00:00:44,890 --> 00:00:45,257
Annie:

21
00:00:45,257 --> 00:00:47,829
Yes, pits were dug in the ground,

22
00:00:47,829 --> 00:00:52,605
and snow was imported from the mountains – even though they were at quite

23
00:00:52,605 --> 00:00:53,340
a distance.

24
00:00:53,340 --> 00:00:55,687
The snow was stored in the pits.

25
00:00:55,687 --> 00:00:58,033
Ice formed at the bottom of it.

26
00:00:58,033 --> 00:01:01,050
Both the ice and the snow were then sold.

27
00:01:01,050 --> 00:01:05,730
The ice cost more than the snow and my guess is that only the

28
00:01:05,730 --> 00:01:08,070
wealthy members of society could afford it.

29
00:01:08,070 --> 00:01:08,451
Jack:

30
00:01:08,451 --> 00:01:09,973
I wouldn't be surprised.

31
00:01:09,973 --> 00:01:13,780
I also came across an article about modern domestic fridges.

32
00:01:13,780 --> 00:01:15,509
Several different technologies are used,

33
00:01:15,509 --> 00:01:18,620
but they were too complex for me to understand.

34
00:01:18,620 --> 00:01:18,877
Annie:

35
00:01:18,877 --> 00:01:22,220
You have to wonder what happens when people get rid of old ones.

36
00:01:22,220 --> 00:01:22,454
Jack:

37
00:01:22,454 --> 00:01:25,260
You mean because the gases in them are harmful for the environment?

38
00:01:25,260 --> 00:01:25,631
Annie:

39
00:01:25,631 --> 00:01:26,002
Exactly.

40
00:01:26,002 --> 00:01:31,196
At least there are now plenty of organisations that will recycle most of the

41
00:01:31,196 --> 00:01:31,938
components safely,

42
00:01:31,938 --> 00:01:36,390
but of course some people just dump old fridges in the countryside.

43
00:01:36,390 --> 00:01:36,696
Jack:

44
00:01:36,696 --> 00:01:39,752
It's hard to see how they can be stopped unfortunately.

45
00:01:39,752 --> 00:01:43,420
In the UK we get rid of three million a year altogether!

46
00:01:43,420 --> 00:01:43,805
Annie:

47
00:01:43,805 --> 00:01:48,040
That sounds a lot, especially because fridges hardly ever break down.

48
00:01:48,040 --> 00:01:48,347
Jack:

49
00:01:48,347 --> 00:01:48,961
That's right.

50
00:01:48,961 --> 00:01:52,644
In this country we keep domestic fridges for 11 years on average,

51
00:01:52,644 --> 00:01:55,100
and a lot last for 20 or more.

52
00:01:55,100 --> 00:01:59,043
So if you divide the cost by the number of years you can use

53
00:01:59,043 --> 00:02:01,860
a fridge, they're not expensive, compared with some household appliances.

54
00:02:01,860 --> 00:02:02,299
Annie:

55
00:02:02,299 --> 00:02:02,739
True.

56
00:02:02,739 --> 00:02:08,451
I suppose manufacturers encourage people to spend more by making them different colours

57
00:02:08,451 --> 00:02:09,330
and designs.

58
00:02:09,330 --> 00:02:14,280
I'm sure when my parents bought their first fridge they had hardly any choice!

59
00:02:14,280 --> 00:02:14,541
Jack:

60
00:02:14,541 --> 00:02:16,110
Yes, there's been quite a change.

61
00:02:16,110 --> 00:02:16,378
Jack:

62
00:02:16,378 --> 00:02:19,597
Right, let's make a list of topics to cover in our presentation,

63
00:02:19,597 --> 00:02:22,280
and decide who's going to do more research on them.

64
00:02:22,280 --> 00:02:25,160
Then later, we can get together and plan the next step.

65
00:02:25,160 --> 00:02:25,621
Annie:

66
00:02:25,621 --> 00:02:26,082
OK.

67
00:02:26,082 --> 00:02:29,771
How about starting with how useful refrigeration is,

68
00:02:29,771 --> 00:02:33,920
and the range of goods that are refrigerated nowadays?

69
00:02:33,920 --> 00:02:37,000
Because of course it's not just food and drinks.

70
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:37,379
Jack:

71
00:02:37,379 --> 00:02:40,790
No, I suppose flowers and medicines are refrigerated, too.

72
00:02:40,790 --> 00:02:41,202
Annie:

73
00:02:41,202 --> 00:02:42,028
And computers.

74
00:02:42,028 --> 00:02:45,740
I could do that, unless you particularly want to.

75
00:02:45,740 --> 00:02:46,082
Jack:

76
00:02:46,082 --> 00:02:46,424
No,

77
00:02:46,424 --> 00:02:47,792
that's fine by me.

78
00:02:47,792 --> 00:02:50,870
What about the effects of refrigeration on people's health?

79
00:02:50,870 --> 00:02:55,379
After all, some of the chemicals used in the 19th century were pretty harmful,

80
00:02:55,379 --> 00:02:59,888
but there have been lots of benefits too, like always having access to fresh

81
00:02:59,888 --> 00:03:00,210
food.

82
00:03:00,210 --> 00:03:01,780
Do you fancy dealing with that?

83
00:03:01,780 --> 00:03:02,111
Annie:

84
00:03:02,111 --> 00:03:04,430
I'm not terribly keen, to be honest.

85
00:03:04,430 --> 00:03:04,771
Jack:

86
00:03:04,771 --> 00:03:05,452
Nor me.

87
00:03:05,452 --> 00:03:09,200
My mind just goes blank when I read anything about chemicals.

88
00:03:09,200 --> 00:03:09,553
Annie:

89
00:03:09,553 --> 00:03:12,733
Oh, all right then, I'll do you a favour.

90
00:03:12,733 --> 00:03:14,500
But you owe me, Jack.

91
00:03:14,500 --> 00:03:14,963
OK.

92
00:03:14,963 --> 00:03:21,452
What about the effects on food producers, like farmers in poorer countries being able

93
00:03:21,452 --> 00:03:24,696
to export their produce to developed countries?

94
00:03:24,696 --> 00:03:26,550
Something for you, maybe?

95
00:03:26,550 --> 00:03:26,849
Jack:

96
00:03:26,849 --> 00:03:27,746
I don't mind.

97
00:03:27,746 --> 00:03:29,240
It should be quite interesting.

98
00:03:29,240 --> 00:03:29,588
Annie:

99
00:03:29,588 --> 00:03:34,107
I think we should also look at how refrigeration has helped whole cities –

100
00:03:34,107 --> 00:03:35,150
like Las Vegas,

101
00:03:35,150 --> 00:03:39,670
which couldn't exist without refrigeration because it's in the middle of a desert.

102
00:03:39,670 --> 00:03:39,925
Jack:

103
00:03:39,925 --> 00:03:40,180
Right.

104
00:03:40,180 --> 00:03:43,753
I had a quick look at an economics book in the library that's got

105
00:03:43,753 --> 00:03:45,540
a chapter about this sort of thing.

106
00:03:45,540 --> 00:03:48,200
I could give you the title, if you want to do this section.

107
00:03:48,200 --> 00:03:48,557
Annie:

108
00:03:48,557 --> 00:03:50,341
Not particularly, to be honest.

109
00:03:50,341 --> 00:03:53,910
I find economics books pretty heavy going, as a rule.

110
00:03:53,910 --> 00:03:54,130
Jack:

111
00:03:54,130 --> 00:03:55,450
OK, leave it to me, then.

112
00:03:55,450 --> 00:03:55,887
Annie:

113
00:03:55,887 --> 00:03:56,323
Thanks.

114
00:03:56,323 --> 00:03:57,633
Then there's transport,

115
00:03:57,633 --> 00:04:01,127
and the difference that refrigerated trucks have made.

116
00:04:01,127 --> 00:04:04,620
I wouldn't mind having a go at that.

117
00:04:04,620 --> 00:04:04,982
Jack:

118
00:04:04,982 --> 00:04:06,430
Don't forget trains, too.

119
00:04:06,430 --> 00:04:11,497
I read something about milk and butter being transported in refrigerated railroad cars in

120
00:04:11,497 --> 00:04:14,030
the USA, right back in the 1840s.

121
00:04:14,030 --> 00:04:14,426
Annie:

122
00:04:14,426 --> 00:04:16,404
I hadn't thought of trains.

123
00:04:16,404 --> 00:04:16,800
Thanks.

124
00:04:16,800 --> 00:04:17,086
Jack:

125
00:04:17,086 --> 00:04:19,665
Shall we have a separate section on domestic fridges?

126
00:04:19,665 --> 00:04:21,670
After all, they're something everyone's familiar with.

127
00:04:21,670 --> 00:04:22,103
Annie:

128
00:04:22,103 --> 00:04:24,701
What about splitting it into two?

129
00:04:24,701 --> 00:04:28,164
You could investigate 19th- and 20th- century fridges,

130
00:04:28,164 --> 00:04:31,628
and I'll concentrate on what's available these days,

131
00:04:31,628 --> 00:04:36,390
and how manufacturers differentiate their products from those of their competitors.

132
00:04:36,390 --> 00:04:36,512
Jack:

133
00:04:36,512 --> 00:04:36,634
OK,

134
00:04:36,634 --> 00:04:37,000
that'd suit me.
